        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Tanzimat</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and the provinces, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a>’s reforms after 1826; opened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-gulhane">Edict of Gülhane</a> (1839); renewed by the Imperial Reform Edict (1856); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilayet">Vilayet</a> Law and the Nizamiye Courts (1864); a period of chaos after Âli Pasha’s death (1871); closed by the constitution of 1876 amid the Great Eastern Crisis (1875–1878).</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-resid-pasha">Mustafa Reşid Pasha</a>, former ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>; the reforming statesmen Mehmed Emin Âli Pasha and Fuad Pasha; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultans</a> Abdul Mejid I and Abdul Aziz; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midhat-pasha">Midhat Pasha</a>, author of the 1876 constitution; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-ottomans">Young Ottomans</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> of reformers.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">A conscripted army, new ministries, schools and universities, secular courts and codes, the Mecelle, railways, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a> and banks, and the constitution of 1876; but also bankruptcy in 1875, foreign control of revenues from 1881, Balkan risings and the losses of the Treaty of Berlin (1878).</span></div>
        </div>
