    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Tanzimat</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii.jpg" alt="Mahmud II" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Mahmud II <span class="kid-tale-cr">Henry Guillaume Schlesinger, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Ottoman Empire, ruled from Istanbul, was losing wars because its armies could not keep up with Europe’s.</p><p>Sultan Mahmud II began to change the way the empire was run, in the style of the West.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>In 1839 the Tanzimat began, an age of reform, or changes meant to make things better.</p><p>A royal order promised every subject safety of life, property and honour, and fair taxes.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace.jpg" alt="Topkapı Palace" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Topkapı Palace <span class="kid-tale-cr">Carlos Delgado, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>The order was named after the rose house in the grounds of the Topkapı Palace.</p><p>Later, a new order promised that people of all religions would be equal before the law.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>The empire built new schools and universities, railways, telegraph lines and banks.</p><p>The slave market of Istanbul was closed in 1847.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/midhat-pasha.jpg" alt="Midhat Pasha" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Midhat Pasha <span class="kid-tale-cr">Atelier Adèle, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Statesmen, more than the sultans, led the changes.</p><p>In 1876 Midhat Pasha wrote a constitution, a set of rules for how the country was to be run.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sublime-porte.jpg" alt="Sublime Porte" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Sublime Porte <span class="kid-tale-cr">Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>But borrowing money left the empire bankrupt, unable to pay its debts, in 1875, and Balkan peoples rose up.</p><p>Historians still argue whether the reforms made the state stronger or helped it break apart.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Launched by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-gulhane">Edict of Gülhane</a> in 1839, the Tanzimat promised all Ottoman subjects security of life, property and honour, fair taxation and conscription, public trials and an end to tax farming.</li><li>Statesmen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sublime-porte">Sublime Porte</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-resid-pasha">Mustafa Reşid Pasha</a>, Âli Pasha and Fuad Pasha rebuilt the ministries, courts, schools, law codes and army, and promoted Ottomanism, the equality of all subjects.</li><li>The reforms culminated in the constitution of 1876 but left the empire bankrupt, while Balkan separatism, conservative opposition and foreign intervention grew; historians still dispute whether they strengthened the state or hastened its break-up.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Ottoman penal code of 1858, modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, abolished punishments such as amputation of a limb for theft and decriminalized homosexuality; it endured until 1928.</p></aside>
    </section>

